<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ CLAUDE INSPIRATION -->
<div class="claude-inspiration theme-teal" id="claude-inspiration">
  <div class="extras-title">🌉 Tides &amp; Trolleys</div>
  <p>San Francisco fits in seven by seven miles, but no two of its hills feel the same. Pause halfway up Telegraph Hill on Day 1 — Filbert Steps slip down the east face in wooden stairs past flagstone gardens and the wild parrots of the Greenwich-Hyde colony. It is the unhurried way back to the Embarcadero, and the photo will outlast the postcards.</p>
  <p>Two practical notes about routing — first, the Powell-Hyde cable-car line passes within two blocks of the Cable Car Museum, so on Day 3 you can ride down to Hyde &amp; Beach if the legs have run out before getting back to the hotel. The Powell turnaround is also the rare cable-car queue worth waiting in early.</p>
  <p>The Capitol Corridor's San Francisco departure point is really a bus bay, with a 30-minute coach ride to Emeryville before the train begins. The single Amtrak ticket covers both legs and the bus is timed to the train — but morning bus seats fill, so arrive 15 minutes early on Day 4.</p>
</div>

